/-
The Lumenary — formal mathematics
The Langlands programme: an orientation. Reciprocity laws from quadratic reciprocity to
cyclotomic fields, Dirichlet's theorem, and modular forms.

Proved here (`@provenance independent`): `-1` is not a square modulo a prime `p ≡ 3 (mod 4)`
(from Fermat's little theorem); an odd prime that is a sum of two squares is `≡ 1 (mod 4)`;
and the reciprocity law for `5`: for odd primes `p ≠ 5`, `5` is a square mod `p` exactly when
`p ≡ ±1 (mod 5)` — derived from quadratic reciprocity.

From Mathlib (`@provenance library`): quadratic reciprocity; Fermat's two-squares theorem;
the Galois group `Gal(ℚ(ζₙ)/ℚ) ≅ (ℤ/nℤ)ˣ`, under which the decomposition group of a prime
`p ∤ n` is generated by `p mod n` (the reciprocity law for cyclotomic fields, i.e. abelian
reciprocity over `ℚ` for these fields); the residue degree of `p` in `ℚ(ζₘ)` is the order of
`p mod m`; Dirichlet's theorem on primes in progressions; the transformation law of modular
forms and the normalisation of the discriminant `Δ`.

Not formalised (and not in Mathlib at this version): class field theory in general, the
Kronecker–Weber theorem, automorphic representations, Galois representations attached to
modular forms, the modularity theorem, functoriality, geometric Langlands.
-/
import Mathlib.NumberTheory.LegendreSymbol.QuadraticReciprocity
import Mathlib.NumberTheory.SumTwoSquares
import Mathlib.NumberTheory.NumberField.Cyclotomic.Galois
import Mathlib.NumberTheory.NumberField.Cyclotomic.Ideal
import Mathlib.NumberTheory.LSeries.PrimesInAP
import Mathlib.NumberTheory.ModularForms.Discriminant

namespace Lumenary.LanglandsProgramme

open NumberField
open scoped MatrixGroups Pointwise

/-! ### Quadratic reciprocity and its consequences -/

-- @region langlands_programme.quadratic_reciprocity
-- @provenance library
/-- **Quadratic reciprocity** (Gauss, 1796): for distinct odd primes `p` and `q`,
`(q/p)(p/q) = (−1)^((p−1)/2 · (q−1)/2)`. (In Lean, `p / 2 = (p − 1)/2` for odd `p`, and
`legendreSym p a` is the Legendre symbol `(a/p)`.) This applies Mathlib's
`legendreSym.quadratic_reciprocity`. -/
theorem quadratic_reciprocity (p q : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] [Fact q.Prime] (hp : p ≠ 2)
    (hq : q ≠ 2) (hpq : p ≠ q) :
    legendreSym q p * legendreSym p q = (-1) ^ (p / 2 * (q / 2)) :=
  legendreSym.quadratic_reciprocity hp hq hpq
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.neg_one_not_square
-- @provenance independent
/-- If `p ≡ 3 (mod 4)` then `−1` is not a square mod `p`. Proof: if `x² = −1` then, by Fermat's
little theorem, `1 = x^(p−1) = (x²)^((p−1)/2) = (−1)^((p−1)/2) = −1`, because `(p − 1)/2` is
odd; so `2 = 0` in `ℤ/pℤ`, i.e. `p ∣ 2`, which is impossible for `p ≡ 3 (mod 4)`.
So `x² + 1` has no root mod `p`: the prime `p` stays prime in `ℤ[i]`. -/
theorem neg_one_not_square (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (hp : p % 4 = 3) :
    ¬ IsSquare (-1 : ZMod p) := by
  rintro ⟨x, hx⟩
  have hx0 : x ≠ 0 := by
    rintro rfl
    simp at hx
  have hp1 : p - 1 = 2 * ((p - 1) / 2) := by omega
  have h1 : x ^ (p - 1) = -1 := by
    rw [hp1, pow_mul, sq, ← hx]
    exact Odd.neg_one_pow ⟨(p - 1) / 2 / 2, by omega⟩
  rw [ZMod.pow_card_sub_one_eq_one hx0] at h1
  have h2 : ((2 : ℕ) : ZMod p) = 0 := by
    push_cast
    linear_combination h1
  rw [ZMod.natCast_eq_zero_iff] at h2
  have := (Nat.prime_dvd_prime_iff_eq Fact.out Nat.prime_two).mp h2
  omega
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.neg_one_square_iff
-- @provenance library
/-- The full splitting law for `x² + 1`: `−1` is a square mod an odd prime `p` iff
`p ≢ 3 (mod 4)`. Whether `x² + 1` factors mod `p` depends only on `p mod 4` — the simplest
reciprocity law. This applies Mathlib's `ZMod.exists_sq_eq_neg_one_iff`. -/
theorem neg_one_square_iff (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] :
    IsSquare (-1 : ZMod p) ↔ p % 4 ≠ 3 :=
  ZMod.exists_sq_eq_neg_one_iff
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.sum_two_squares_mod_four
-- @provenance independent
/-- An odd prime that is a sum of two squares is `≡ 1 (mod 4)`: squares are `0` or `1` mod `4`,
so a sum of two squares is never `3` mod `4`. (A prime `p = a² + b² = (a + bi)(a − bi)` splits in
`ℤ[i]`.) -/
theorem sum_two_squares_mod_four (p a b : ℕ) (hp : p.Prime) (hp2 : p ≠ 2)
    (h : a ^ 2 + b ^ 2 = p) : p % 4 = 1 := by
  have hodd : p % 2 = 1 := (hp.eq_two_or_odd).resolve_left hp2
  have : (a ^ 2 + b ^ 2) % 4 ≠ 3 := by
    have ha := Nat.mod_lt a (by norm_num : 4 > 0)
    have hb := Nat.mod_lt b (by norm_num : 4 > 0)
    rw [Nat.add_mod, Nat.pow_mod, Nat.pow_mod b]
    interval_cases (a % 4) <;> interval_cases (b % 4) <;> norm_num
  omega
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.fermat_two_squares
-- @provenance library
/-- **Fermat's two-squares theorem** (stated by Fermat, proved by Euler): a prime `p ≢ 3 (mod 4)`
is a sum of two squares. With `sum_two_squares_mod_four`, a prime splits in `ℤ[i]` iff
`p ≡ 1 (mod 4)` (or `p = 2`, which ramifies). This applies Mathlib's `Nat.Prime.sq_add_sq`. -/
theorem fermat_two_squares (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (hp : p % 4 ≠ 3) :
    ∃ a b : ℕ, a ^ 2 + b ^ 2 = p :=
  Nat.Prime.sq_add_sq hp
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.fact_prime_five
-- @provenance definition
/-- The instance recording that `5` is prime, so that `ZMod 5` is a field and `legendreSym 5` makes
sense. -/
instance fact_prime_five : Fact (Nat.Prime 5) := ⟨Nat.prime_five⟩
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.five_square_iff
-- @provenance independent
/-- **A reciprocity law with conductor 5.** For a prime `p ≠ 2, 5`: `5` is a square mod `p` iff
`p ≡ ±1 (mod 5)`. Whether `x² − 5` factors mod `p` is decided by a congruence on `p` — the
splitting law of `ℚ(√5) ⊂ ℚ(ζ₅)`. Proof: since `5 ≡ 1 (mod 4)`, quadratic reciprocity gives
`(5/p) = (p/5)`; this depends only on `p mod 5`, and the squares mod `5` are `1` and `4`. -/
theorem five_square_iff (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (hp2 : p ≠ 2) (hp5 : p ≠ 5) :
    IsSquare ((5 : ℕ) : ZMod p) ↔ p % 5 = 1 ∨ p % 5 = 4 := by
  have hne : ((5 : ℕ) : ZMod p) ≠ 0 := by
    rw [Ne, ZMod.natCast_eq_zero_iff]
    intro h
    exact hp5 ((Nat.prime_dvd_prime_iff_eq Fact.out Nat.prime_five).mp h)
  rw [← legendreSym.eq_one_iff' p hne]
  -- reciprocity: `(5/p) = (p/5)`
  have hqr : legendreSym p ((5 : ℕ) : ℤ) = legendreSym 5 (p : ℤ) := by
    exact_mod_cast legendreSym.quadratic_reciprocity_one_mod_four (by norm_num : 5 % 4 = 1) hp2
  rw [hqr, legendreSym.mod 5 (p : ℤ)]
  have hcast : (p : ℤ) % ((5 : ℕ) : ℤ) = ((p % 5 : ℕ) : ℤ) := by push_cast; rfl
  rw [hcast]
  have hr : p % 5 = 1 ∨ p % 5 = 2 ∨ p % 5 = 3 ∨ p % 5 = 4 := by
    have h5 : ¬ 5 ∣ p := fun h =>
      hp5 ((Nat.prime_dvd_prime_iff_eq Nat.prime_five Fact.out).mp h).symm
    omega
  have hne' : ((p % 5 : ℕ) : ZMod 5) ≠ 0 := by
    rcases hr with h | h | h | h <;> rw [h] <;> decide
  rw [legendreSym.eq_one_iff' 5 hne']
  -- the squares mod 5 are 1 and 4: a finite check
  rcases hr with h | h | h | h <;> rw [h] <;> decide
-- @endregion

/-! ### Cyclotomic fields: abelian reciprocity over `ℚ` -/

-- @region langlands_programme.cyclotomic_galois
-- @provenance definition
/-- The isomorphism `Gal(ℚ(ζₙ)/ℚ) ≅ (ℤ/nℤ)ˣ`, sending `σ` to the class `a` with `σ(ζₙ) = ζₙᵃ`.
It exists because the cyclotomic polynomial `Φₙ` is irreducible over `ℚ`. This is Mathlib's
`IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.galEquivZMod`. -/
noncomputable def cyclotomicGalois (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (K : Type*) [Field K] [NumberField K]
    [IsCyclotomicExtension {n} ℚ K] : Gal(K/ℚ) ≃* (ZMod n)ˣ :=
  IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.galEquivZMod n K
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.cyclotomic_frobenius
-- @provenance library
/-- **Reciprocity for cyclotomic fields.** Let `p ∤ n` be prime and `P` a prime of `ℚ(ζₙ)` above
`p`. Under `Gal(ℚ(ζₙ)/ℚ) ≅ (ℤ/nℤ)ˣ`, the decomposition group of `P` is the cyclic subgroup
generated by `p mod n`: the Frobenius element at `p` "is" `p mod n`. This is class field theory
for the fields `ℚ(ζₙ)`, and by Kronecker–Weber every abelian extension of `ℚ` lies in one of
them. This applies Mathlib's `IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.galEquivZMod_stabilizer`. -/
theorem cyclotomic_frobenius {n : ℕ} [NeZero n] (K : Type*) [Field K] [NumberField K]
    [IsCyclotomicExtension {n} ℚ K] (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (P : Ideal (𝓞 K)) [P.IsMaximal]
    [P.LiesOver (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)})] (hn : p.Coprime n) :
    (IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.galEquivZMod n K).mapSubgroup (MulAction.stabilizer Gal(K/ℚ) P) =
      Subgroup.zpowers (ZMod.unitOfCoprime p hn) :=
  IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.galEquivZMod_stabilizer n K p P hn
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.cyclotomic_residue_degree
-- @provenance library
/-- For a prime `p ∤ m`, the residue degree of `p` in `ℚ(ζₘ)` is the multiplicative order of
`p mod m`: `p` factors into `φ(m)/f` primes of degree `f = ord_m(p)`. This applies Mathlib's
`IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.inertiaDegIn_eq_of_not_dvd`. -/
theorem cyclotomic_residue_degree {m : ℕ} [NeZero m] (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (K : Type*)
    [Field K] [NumberField K] [IsCyclotomicExtension {m} ℚ K] (hm : ¬ p ∣ m) :
    (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).inertiaDegIn (𝓞 K) = orderOf (p : ZMod m) :=
  IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.inertiaDegIn_eq_of_not_dvd p K hm
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.cyclotomic_unramified
-- @provenance library
/-- A prime `p ∤ m` is unramified in `ℚ(ζₘ)`. This applies Mathlib's
`IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.ramificationIdxIn_eq_of_not_dvd`. -/
theorem cyclotomic_unramified {m : ℕ} [NeZero m] (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (K : Type*)
    [Field K] [NumberField K] [IsCyclotomicExtension {m} ℚ K] (hm : ¬ p ∣ m) :
    (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).ramificationIdxIn (𝓞 K) = 1 :=
  IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.ramificationIdxIn_eq_of_not_dvd p K hm
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.splits_completely_iff
-- @provenance library
/-- The splitting law of `ℚ(ζₘ)`: a prime `p ∤ m` (unramified by `cyclotomic_unramified`) has
residue degree `1` — it **splits completely** — iff `p ≡ 1 (mod m)`. A one-line corollary of
Mathlib's `IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.inertiaDegIn_eq_of_not_dvd`, using that an element of a
group has order `1` iff it is the identity (`orderOf_eq_one_iff`). -/
theorem splits_completely_iff {m : ℕ} [NeZero m] (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (K : Type*) [Field K]
    [NumberField K] [IsCyclotomicExtension {m} ℚ K] (hm : ¬ p ∣ m) :
    (Ideal.span {(p : ℤ)}).inertiaDegIn (𝓞 K) = 1 ↔ (p : ZMod m) = 1 := by
  rw [IsCyclotomicExtension.Rat.inertiaDegIn_eq_of_not_dvd p K hm, orderOf_eq_one_iff]
-- @endregion

/-! ### Automorphic forms for `GL(1)` and `GL(2)` -/

-- @region langlands_programme.dirichlet_primes_in_ap
-- @provenance library
/-- **Dirichlet's theorem** (1837): every invertible residue class `a mod q` contains infinitely
many primes. Its proof is the non-vanishing of `L(1, χ)` for the Dirichlet characters `χ mod q`
— the automorphic forms for `GL(1)` over `ℚ`. In Galois terms, every element of
`Gal(ℚ(ζ_q)/ℚ)` is a Frobenius element infinitely often (the abelian case of Chebotarev).
This applies Mathlib's `Nat.forall_exists_prime_gt_and_eq_mod`. -/
theorem dirichlet_primes_in_ap {q : ℕ} [NeZero q] {a : ZMod q} (ha : IsUnit a) (n : ℕ) :
    ∃ p > n, p.Prime ∧ (p : ZMod q) = a :=
  Nat.forall_exists_prime_gt_and_eq_mod ha n
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.modular_transformation
-- @provenance library
/-- The defining symmetry of a **modular form** of weight `k` for `SL(2, ℤ)`:
`f((az + b)/(cz + d)) = (cz + d)^k f(z)` for every `γ = (a b; c d) ∈ SL(2, ℤ)` and every `z` in
the upper half-plane (`denom γ z = cz + d`). In Mathlib a `ModularForm 𝒮ℒ k` is a holomorphic
function on `ℍ` with this symmetry and bounded at the cusp. This applies
`SlashInvariantForm.slash_action_eqn''`. -/
theorem modular_transformation {k : ℤ} (f : ModularForm 𝒮ℒ k) (γ : SL(2, ℤ))
    (z : UpperHalfPlane) :
    f (γ • z) = (UpperHalfPlane.denom γ z) ^ k * f z :=
  SlashInvariantForm.slash_action_eqn'' f (by exact ⟨γ, rfl⟩) z
-- @endregion

-- @region langlands_programme.discriminant_first_coefficient
-- @provenance library
/-- Ramanujan's cusp form `Δ(z) = q ∏ₙ (1 − qⁿ)²⁴ = Σ τ(n) qⁿ` (weight 12, level 1) is
normalised: its `q`-expansion starts `q + …`, i.e. `τ(1) = 1`. Mathlib defines `Δ` from the
Dedekind eta function and proves it is a cusp form. This applies
`ModularForm.discriminant_qExpansion_coeff_one`. The multiplicativity of `τ` (Mordell, 1917)
and Deligne's bound `|τ(p)| ≤ 2p^(11/2)` (1974) are not in Mathlib. -/
theorem discriminant_first_coefficient :
    (PowerSeries.coeff 1) (UpperHalfPlane.qExpansion 1 ModularForm.discriminant) = 1 :=
  ModularForm.discriminant_qExpansion_coeff_one
-- @endregion

end Lumenary.LanglandsProgramme
